    <DataNode elementId="AT2G40840-MONOMER" textLabel="4-alpha-glucanotransferase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q8RXD9" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="535" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">|FRAME: AT2G40840-MONOMER DPE2| (disproportionating enzyme 2) is a cytosolic enzyme involved in metabolizing maltose exported from the chloroplast during starch degradation. &lt;i&gt;dpe2&lt;/i&gt; mutant plants have higher levels of starch at the end of the night than wild type plants and they also have elevated levels of maltose and decreased levels of sucrose overall which is consistent with the proposed role for |FRAME: AT2G40840-MONOMER DPE2| in |FRAME: PWY-7238 sucrose biosynthesis| |CITS: [14996213]|. As an &amp;alpha;-glucanotransferase, |FRAME: AT2G40840-MONOMER DPE2| transfers a donor glycosyl unit to an acceptor, releasing glucose in the process. Early examinations of this enzyme showed that it could use the non-physiologically relevant glycogen as an acceptor |CITS: [14996213]|. Further &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; experiments show that it can use glucose, glycogen, and maltose as a glycosyl donor and can act on a wide variety of substrates including monosaccharides (such as |FRAME: Glucose glucose|, |FRAME: MANNOSE D-mannose| and |FRAME: D-Xylose D-xylose|) and maltodextrins such as |FRAME: MALTOTRIOSE maltotriose| |CITS: [16640603]|. However, several lines of evidence now suggest that the poorly defined class of compounds referred to as |FRAME: Soluble-Heteroglycans Soluble HeteroGlycans| (SHG) likely serve as the physiologically relevant acceptor of DPE2-donated glucosyl units in the cytosol |CITS: [16640603]|. And, there is also evidence that |FRAME: AT2G40840-MONOMER DPE2| uses the |FRAME: MALTOSE maltose| anomeric form of maltose produced from |FRAME: PWY-6724 starch degradation in the chloroplast| as its preferred substrate |CITS: [17028209]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G40840-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G40840.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q8RXD9" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33752, RXN-21550, RXN-11734, RXN-10217, RXN-7347" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="5" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT2G40840.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT2G40840" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_14996213" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_16640603" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_17028209" />
    </DataNode>
